{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/reader-packet.md'","aggregated_output":"# Blind reading packet: six answers to one judging request (FY2027 Data Center revenue, NVIDIA, 2026-09-20)\n\nEach answer below was written for the same question on the same day. Some requests also showed the total-revenue question's\nconclusion (quoted below); you are not told which. Apply `reading-rule.md` (A to E) to each answer.\n\n## v14 (FY2027 total revenue), its 09-20 conclusion\nrung 2; reasons: The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for “next year” and is not quantified as a reduction to FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.\n\n## answer-P\n- rung 3; reference: Approximately $414 billion for FY2027, the market reference supplied in the previous conclusion; this is rung 2 (at least $400 billion and below $450 billion).; implied outcome: About $384–390 billion: approximately $164 billion in the first half, about $100 billion in Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent 92.5% Data Center mix, plus roughly $120–126 billion in Q4 as supply builds and hyperscale growth reaccelerates.\n- cites_views: []; cites: ['e74', 'e153', 'e2510', 'e2562', 'e2567', 'e2572', 'e2574', 'e2848', 'e3219', 'e3222', 'e3234', 'e3805', 'e4052', 'e4250', 'e4331', 'e4556', 'e4640', 'e4765', 'e4779', 'e4877', 'e4895', 'e4995']\n- reasons: The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The evidence implies about $384–390 billion: Q1 and Q2 total about $164 billion; applying the latest Data Center mix to NVIDIA’s $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide implies about $100 billion for Q3; and management’s Q4 comments support roughly $120–126 billion as Rubin supply builds and hyperscale growth reaccelerates. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary and within rung 3. Demand and customer commitments support growth, but the no-China-revenue outlook, supply constraints, and earlier competition weigh against lifting the estimate above that boundary.\n- why changed: The rung remains 3, but newer evidence changes the supporting balance: additional quantified customer demand and the updated H200-authorization theme add upside, while Huawei’s accelerated product timing and further competition add downside. These do not move the implied FY2027 total across the $400 billion boundary.\n\n## answer-Q\n- rung 3; reference: Approximately $414 billion for FY2027, the supplied market reference; this sits at rung 2 (at least $400 billion and below $450 billion).; implied outcome: About $384–389 billion: the first two quarters total about $164 billion, Q3 is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds.\n- cites_views: ['v14']; cites: ['e4995', 'e4996', 'e2848', 'e3219', 'e3222', 'e2567', 'e2572', 'e2574', 'e2541', 'e4052', 'e4765', 'e2562', 'e2471', 'e4640', 'e976', 'e109', 'e127', 'e153', 'e2510', 'e4250', 'e4311', 'e4555', 'e4556', 'e4557', 'e4558', 'e4572', 'e4725', 'e4185', 'e3805', 'e4996']\n- reasons: The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The operating evidence still implies a lower result: the first half contributed about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is about $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent segment mix, and Q4 is roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds. That yields about $384–389 billion, below the $400 billion boundary. Strong demand and the Rubin ramp support growth, but the evidence does not quantify enough Q4 revenue to bridge the gap to rung 2; the segment estimate is also consistent with the dependent FY2027 total-revenue conclusion.\n- why changed: The rung and the key supporting estimate remain materially unchanged from the previous judgment: approximately $164 billion in the first half, about $100 billion in Q3, and roughly $120–125 billion in Q4. The newly explicit first-half figure confirms, rather than revises, the prior estimate.\n\n## answer-R\n- rung 3; reference: The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2.; implied outcome: About $389–394 billion: $164.25 billion reported in Q1–Q2, approximately $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $125–130 billion for Q4 by carrying forward guided growth while allowing for the stated Q4 acceleration as Rubin supply builds.\n- cites_views: []; cites: ['e74', 'e2517', 'e2574', 'e2848', 'e3219', 'e3222', 'e2567', 'e2572', 'e4052', 'e4250', 'e4330', 'e4331', 'e4571', 'e4572', 'e4765', 'e4995', 'e4996', 'e109', 'e127', 'e153', 'e3805', 'e4534', 'e2562']\n- reasons: The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The implied outcome is about $389–394 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue totals $164.25 billion; applying the latest total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix implies about $100 billion in Q3; and carrying the guided sequential growth forward, with an allowance for the stated Q4 acceleration as Rubin supply builds, implies roughly $125–130 billion in Q4. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and the Rubin ramp lift the estimate versus the prior conclusion, but the Q4 acceleration is not quantified and the evidence also indicates supply constraints; the reported first-half annualized run rate alone is not a full-year forecast.\n- why changed: The rung is unchanged, but the key supporting facts and implied range have changed with the reported Q2 result and newer guidance and demand evidence; the estimate is now about $389–394 billion rather than the prior $385–390 billion.\n\n## answer-S\n- rung 3; reference: About $411 billion of FY2027 total NVIDIA revenue, which is rung 2 for the total-revenue thesis; at the latest Data Center mix of roughly 92.5%, that implies about $380 billion of Data Center revenue, rung 3.; implied outcome: About $387–390 billion: H1 Data Center revenue was about $164 billion; applying the latest roughly 92.5% Data Center mix to v14's estimated Q3 total revenue of about $113.5 billion and Q4 total revenue of about $127 billion gives roughly $105 billion in Q3 and $118 billion in Q4. The result is below $400 billion and above $350 billion.\n- cites_views: ['v14']; cites: ['e74', 'e2574', 'e4995', 'e4803', 'e2567', 'e2541', 'e3222', 'e2510', 'e2562', 'e2572', 'e2471', 'e109', 'e127', 'e976']\n- reasons: The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 total NVIDIA revenue; applying the latest Data Center mix of roughly 92.5% puts the Data Center reference near $380 billion, at rung 3. The implied Data Center outcome is modestly higher, but remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary: H1 was about $164 billion, with Q3 and Q4 estimates of roughly $105 billion and $118 billion, respectively. Strong demand and the Rubin ramp support those estimates, while supply constraints and the lack of a quantified FY2027 contribution from multiyear deployment plans limit the case for crossing $400 billion.\n- why changed: The rung is unchanged, but the current estimate uses the updated FY2027 total-revenue run-rate in v14 and the latest reported H1 Data Center revenue and mix to refresh the quarterly build. This leaves the implied result near $387–390 billion, within the previous estimate's range, rather than changing the rung.\n\n## answer-T\n- rung 3; reference: The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, placing it at rung 2 (at least $400 billion and below $450 billion).; implied outcome: About $389–394 billion: $164.25 billion reported in Q1–Q2, approximately $99.9 billion for Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, plus an estimated $125–130 billion in Q4 as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds.\n- cites_views: []; cites: ['e74', 'e2574', 'e4995', 'e2848', 'e3219', 'e3222', 'e2541', 'e2567', 'e2572', 'e3234', 'e4331', 'e4572', 'e4556', 'e2510', 'e2562', 'e153', 'e109', 'e976', 'e4574']\n- reasons: The market reference remains approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence now implies about $389–394 billion: Q1 and Q2 contributed $164.25 billion, Q3 is about $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is roughly $125–130 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds. That estimate is higher than the previous $385–390 billion view, but remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Strong demand, Rubin orders, and customer utilization support the upper end; the 2027–2028 nature of some commitments, supply constraints, and no China compute revenue in the forward outlook limit the uplift.\n- why changed: The rung remains 3, but the implied FY2027 estimate has moved up from the previous $385–390 billion range to about $389–394 billion, reflecting the reported first-half total and the latest demand and Rubin-ramp evidence.\n\n## answer-U\n- rung 3; reference: Approximately $414 billion for FY2027, the supplied market reference; this sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion).; implied outcome: Approximately $382 billion: $164.25 billion reported in the first half, about $99.9 billion implied for Q3, and about $117.5 billion for Q4 by applying the recent 92.5% Data Center mix to v14’s roughly $127 billion Q4 total-revenue estimate.\n- cites_views: ['v14']; cites: ['e74', 'e2517', 'e2848', 'e3216', 'e3219', 'e3222', 'e2541', 'e2572', 'e4995']\n- reasons: The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The reported first-half Data Center revenue is about $164.25 billion; Q3 is implied at about $99.9 billion using NVIDIA’s $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent 92.5% Data Center mix. For Q4, applying that mix to the roughly $127 billion total-revenue estimate in v14 gives about $117.5 billion, for implied FY2027 Data Center revenue of approximately $382 billion. That is below the $400 billion boundary for rung 2 and within rung 3. Rubin shipments, orders, and expected Q4 hyperscaler reacceleration support upside, but do not provide a quantified FY2027 increment sufficient to bridge the gap; the reported supply constraint is a counterweight. The implied Data Center total also remains below v14’s whole-company revenue estimate.\n- why changed: The rung remains 3, but the implied figure is updated: using the current total-revenue run-rate conclusion in v14 and the recent Data Center mix yields about $382 billion, rather than the previous estimate of roughly $385–390 billion.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"awk '/\"'^#+ The reading rule([[:space:]]|$)/ {active=1; print; next} /''^#+ / {active=0} active || /Reconciles/ {print}'\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/reading-rule.md\"","aggregated_output":"## The reading rule (fixed before any answer)\nFor each answer, two readers (Claude first, then you blind to the arm under shuffled labels, given the answer, v14's 09-20 conclusion\nand the request's evidence) record:\n- **A.** Its FY2027 Data Center estimate and its Q3 and Q4 FY2027 Data Center estimates, as stated or as its own arithmetic gives them.\n- **B.** Whether its arithmetic adds up (its quarters sum to its year within $2B).\n- **C.** Whether each assumption is disclosed and grounded in its own request (a figure, guidance or growth rate that request\n  contains: the evidence listed, and v14's shown conclusion only in the changed arm); a step with no source in its request is\n  unsupported.\n- **D.** Room for the rest of NVIDIA: v14's figures minus the Data Center figures leave at least $25B for FY2027 and at least $6B\n  in each of Q3 and Q4. These floors are this test's assumptions, taken from the reported non-Data-Center revenue in the request\n  (Q1 $6.35B, Q2 $7.2B, first half $13.55B). Both arms are measured against v14's 09-20 figures ($419B FY, $113.5B Q3, $127B Q4),\n  whether or not the answer saw them.\n- **E.** (changed arm only, apart) whether v14's forecast bounds or changes its calculation (a v14 figure used in its arithmetic or\n  as a stated ceiling); a citation or a mention alone is recorded as \"noticed\", not \"used\".\n\n**Reconciles** = A gives all three figures, B, C and D all hold. A missing Q3 or Q4 estimate = \"not tested\", never a pass.\n\n- **Pass:** changed reconciles in at least 2 of 3; control reconciles in at most 1 of 3; no added refusal (first attempts compared).\n- **Inconclusive:** control reconciles in 2 or 3 of 3 (the stored contradiction is a coin flip here), or changed has 2 or more\n  \"not tested\".\n- **Fail:** otherwise. A fail says this input did not reliably help; it does not locate the cause.\n- Where the two readers differ on a point, the source decides, both marks recorded.\n\n**What it decides:** pass → reopen the dependency design review (D542's five items, the `reads`/`supports` separation, D538's seven\nfiling changes) with the council, then Alex; fail or inconclusive → report to Alex, no further send without a new design.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/control-request-user.txt'","aggregated_output":"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 123 items]\n[Not shown: 1164 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169, e278, e602, e680, e771, e1708, e1716, e1790; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA’s AI computing products.\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\n- e127 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently, potentially reducing demand for NVIDIA accelerators in China.\n    Quote: Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e153 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e636 [party_statement; AMD told CNBC; original source AMD ; from gnews:b79717064762299fc9426a13 2026-07-20] AMD said it plans to book tens of billions of dollars in data-center AI revenue starting in 2027, with most of it from Helios.\n    Quote: AMD told CNBC that it plans to book tens of billions in data center AI revenue starting in 2027, the majority coming from Helios.\n- e976 [third_party_view; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n    Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\n- e2115 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar estimates NVIDIA Data Center revenue will exceed $300 billion in FY2027 and perhaps $500 billion in FY2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia should generate well over $300 billion of data center revenue in calendar 2026, which is effectively fiscal 2027, and perhaps over $500 billion in fiscal 2028.\n- e2471 [third_party_view; CNBC's Jim Cramer; original source Jim Cramer 2026-08-25; from gnews:1218d24ec31f2081cea0f597 2026-08-25] Jim Cramer said high-bandwidth memory shortages are costing NVIDIA sales; no period or amount was specified.\n    Quote: shortages of high-bandwidth memory that are costing the company sales\n- e2510 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Amazon Web Services 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:aws-and-nvidia-to-deliver-2-million-additional-gpus-and-next-generation-infrastructure-for-agentic-and-physical-ai 2026-08-26] AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra GPUs across its global infrastructure in 2027–2028.\n    Quote: AWS plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra GPUs in 2027-2028 across AWS Global Infrastructure, including AI factories.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2536, e2839; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2517 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago\n- e2541 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA began production shipments of Vera Rubin in August 2026 and said it had purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\n    Quote: We commenced production shipments of Vera Rubin earlier this month, having already received purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, we expect Vera Rubin to mark the fastest product ramp in NVIDIA's history.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2631, e2731, e2793, e3246, e3387; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e503 (2026-07-16); both are in force: Tom's Hardware reported that Rubin racks were expected to reach volume production in the second half of 2026; NVIDIA said the Noetra facility would support trillion-parameter model training as it expands, suggesting a phased ramp.\n- e2562 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA's forward outlook included no China Data Center compute revenue.\n    Quote: And given ongoing geopolitical uncertainty, there is no China data center compute revenue in our forward outlook.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2349, e3676; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2567 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA expected Vera Rubin to account for approximately 20% of Q3 FY2027 Data Center revenue.\n    Quote: We see Vera Rubin accounting for about 20% of data center revenue in Q3.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2732, e3500, e3974; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2572 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said its available supply supported approximately 70% of expected FY2028 growth, while demand was substantially higher.\n    Quote: we have supply for 70%. We have more supply than 70%, but about 70%. Our demand is much higher than that.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2696, e2739, e3526; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2574 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000073:q2fy27cfocommentary.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported second-quarter FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, driven by the Blackwell Ultra ramp.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue for the second quarter was a record $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago and up 18% sequentially, driven by the ramp of our Blackwell Ultra infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2608; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2844 [party_statement; techcrunch.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] Amazon reported that its custom-chip business exceeded a $25 billion annualized revenue run rate, driven by $225 billion in total commitments from AI labs including Anthropic and OpenAI.\n    Quote: Amazon has said its custom chip business is growing, noting on its last earnings call that it crossed a $25 billion annualized revenue run rate, driven by $225 billion in total commitments from AI labs like Anthropic and OpenAI.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4323, e4324; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2848 [party_statement; techcrunch.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided to third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, with some revenue expected from Rubin GPUs.\n    Quote: Nvidia said it expects revenue to reach $108 billion in the third quarter, some of which will come from its next-gen Rubin GPUs.\n- e3216 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source fool.com (Daniel Sparks) 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] NVIDIA Data Center revenue rose from 87.9% of total revenue in FY2026 Q2 to 92.5% in FY2027 Q2, increasing each quarter.\n    Quote: A year ago, in the second quarter of fiscal 2026, data center revenue was 87.9% of Nvidia's total. The share rose to 89.8% the next quarter, then 91.5%, then 92.2%, then 92.5% in the quarter just reported.\n- e3219 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source fool.com (Daniel Sparks) 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] Daniel Sparks calculated that FY2027 Q3 Data Center revenue would be $99.9 billion if its share of total revenue stayed at FY2027 Q2's 92.5%.\n    Quote: Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\n- e3222 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] NVIDIA said hyperscale-business growth should reaccelerate in FY2027 Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n    Quote: And the company said growth in its hyperscale business (sales to the giant cloud computing providers and the biggest consumer internet companies) should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n- e3234 [party_statement; Andy Jassy, quoted by fool.com; original source Andy Jassy ; from gnews:6eec748064565c1339694577 2026-08-28] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said Amazon would lack capacity to meet all demand in 2026 and expected that constraint to continue in 2027; he said demand already visible for 2028 was striking.\n    Quote: “Even at that amount, we will still not have enough capacity to meet all the demand we have in 2026, and I believe this dynamic will also be true in 2027, too,” Jassy said on the call. “In fact, the demand we already have for 2028 is striking.”\n- e3805 [third_party_view; theguardian.com; original source theguardian.com 2026-09-03; from gnews:774c4b3a90a519fc0d3c9024 2026-09-03] The Guardian reported that Meta, OpenAI, and Microsoft are developing their own AI chips to reduce reliance on NVIDIA processors, and said this could contribute to slower demand for NVIDIA chips.\n    Quote: For Nvidia, building up its open source business may help it cushion a demand slowdown from customers such as ⁠Meta, OpenAI and Microsoft, which are developing their own AI chips to cut reliance ​on its costly and ​supply-constrained processors.\n- e3975 [third_party_view; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source Adam Spatacco 2026-09-04; from gnews:bbd72d5fbff98d39746e6cb9 2026-09-04] The author estimated that Vera Rubin could generate close to $20 billion in sales in its first real quarter of shipments.\n    Quote: Considering that Nvidia's data center segment makes up more than 90% of the company's total revenue, it's reasonable to forecast Vera Rubin being on track for something close to $20 billion of sales in its first real quarter of shipments.\n- e4052 [party_statement; calcalistech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] NVIDIA forecast most FY2027 Q3 data-center growth to come from ACIE, with hyperscale growth accelerating in Q4 and through FY2028 as Vera Rubin supply expands.\n    Quote: According to Nvidia’s forecast, most of the growth in the third quarter is expected to come from ACIE, while growth in hyperscale is expected to accelerate again in the fourth quarter and throughout fiscal 2028 as the supply of the Vera Rubin platform expands.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4765; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4185 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:91e29a08de15b30c95cb66ac 2026-09-07] Broadcom raised its forecast for AI chip revenue in the fiscal year ending October 2027 to about $115 billion, from a prior forecast of over $100 billion.\n    Quote: it now expects AI chip revenue of about $115 billion in the fiscal year ending October 2027, up from a prior forecast of over $100 billion.\n- e4250 [party_statement; Michael Intrator, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Michael Intrator, CoreWeave CEO ; from gnews:f22b97377c3d9832608a0fc8 2026-09-09] CoreWeave CEO Michael Intrator said the company was struggling to meet demand for GPUs and could sell each GPU it had to multiple clients.\n    Quote: \"We are struggling to meet demand every day,\" CoreWeave CEO Michael Intrator told Yahoo Finance from the Goldman Sachs Communacopia & Tech Conference (video above). \"Every GPU we have could be sold to multiple different clients. It is a unique moment, and it continues to be.\"\n- e4254 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source CoreWeave management ; from gnews:f22b97377c3d9832608a0fc8 2026-09-09] CoreWeave management raised its 2026 total revenue guidance to $12.4 billion–$13.2 billion, citing enterprise GPU-compute demand and a $104 billion total revenue backlog.\n    Quote: Driven by explosive enterprise demand for graphics processing unit (GPU) compute and a massive $104 billion total revenue backlog, management raised its full-year 2026 revenue guidance to a range of $12.4 billion to $13.2 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4311 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:ab2afd5299068837dcf8eae0 2026-09-10] Jensen Huang said orders for NVIDIA’s system combining 36 Grace CPUs with 72 Blackwell GPUs were growing 27% month over month.\n    Quote: He added that orders for just one product, a computer system that combines 36 Grace CPUs with 72 Blackwell GPUs, is currently experiencing 27% month-to-month sales growth.\n- e4330 [party_statement; Benzinga, reporting Oracle CEO Clay Magouyrk's fiscal 2027 first-quarter earnings-call remarks; original source Clay Magouyrk, Oracle CEO ; from gnews:44f4bd6cc91ff33688f1e69f 2026-09-10] Oracle CEO Clay Magouyrk said Oracle had delivered 850 MW of AI capacity containing more than 300,000 GPUs since the end of the previous quarter.\n    Quote: Magouyrk also said the company delivered 850 megawatts of AI capacity containing more than 300,000 GPUs since the end of its previous quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4562; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4331 [fact; Benzinga; original source Oracle ; from gnews:44f4bd6cc91ff33688f1e69f 2026-09-10] Oracle closed more than $30 billion in additional AI contracts during its fiscal 2027 first quarter without requiring additional capital; GPU utilization was 97.9%.\n    Quote: Oracle also closed more than $30 billion in additional AI contracts during the quarter without requiring additional capital, while GPU utilization remained extremely high at 97.9%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4537, e4563, e4566; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4534 [party_statement; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source OpenAI ; from gnews:ab387b35d7ed1e8494a84d33 2026-09-12] OpenAI said its Jalapeño chip, co-developed with Broadcom, is intended to run large AI models more efficiently and is planned for deployment across very large data centers; Reuters reported that reducing reliance on NVIDIA GPUs and operating costs was part of the motivation.\n    Quote: According to Reuters, OpenAI developed the chip partly to reduce its reliance on Nvidia GPUs and lower operating costs. OpenAI said that Jalapeño was built specifically to run large AI models more efficiently and is planned for deployment across very large data centers.\n- e4555 [party_statement; Elon Musk, quoted from SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:6490e4ac2a408fad6a7e99a1 2026-09-12] SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX has decided to build exclusively on Nvidia going forward, citing Vera Rubin and its relationship with Nvidia.\n    Quote: [G]oing forward, we've decided to build exclusively on Nvidia because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture. We think it's the best AI computer, and we greatly value our close cooperation and partnership on many levels with Nvidia. So we're exclusive to Nvidia.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3513; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4556 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Anthropic and SpaceX ; from gnews:6490e4ac2a408fad6a7e99a1 2026-09-12] Anthropic entered into a SpaceX cloud-services agreement worth $1.25 billion per month through 2029 for access to compute across supercomputers featuring roughly 325,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Anthropic entered into a cloud services agreement with SpaceX worth $1.25 billion a month through 2029. The deal provides Anthropic with access to compute capacity across SpaceX's supercomputers, Colossus and Colossus II, which feature roughly 325,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4557 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Google Cloud ; from gnews:6490e4ac2a408fad6a7e99a1 2026-09-12] Google Cloud committed $920 million per month through mid-2029 for access to roughly 110,000 Nvidia GPUs, CPUs, memory, and related networking equipment.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Google Cloud committed $920 million a month for access to roughly 110,000 Nvidia GPUs, CPUs, memory, and related networking kits through the middle of 2029.\n- e4558 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Reflection AI ; from gnews:6490e4ac2a408fad6a7e99a1 2026-09-12] Reflection AI signed a multiyear agreement worth up to $6.3 billion to access Nvidia GB300 chips, at a reported $150 million per month.\n    Quote: Most recently, Reflection AI signed a multiyear deal worth up to $6.3 billion to access Nvidia GB300 chips at a reported $150 million per month.\n- e4562 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Oracle ; from gnews:0c19765ee99d1f808e0834ec 2026-09-12] Oracle delivered 850 megawatts of AI capacity containing more than 300,000 GPUs to customers in fiscal Q1 2027, ended August 31.\n    Quote: The software and cloud giant delivered 850 megawatts of artificial intelligence (AI) capacity to customers in its fiscal first quarter of 2027 (the period ended Aug. 31). That hardware contained more than 300,000 graphics processing units (GPUs).\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4330; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4563 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Oracle ; from gnews:0c19765ee99d1f808e0834ec 2026-09-12] Oracle reported 97.9% utilization across its GPU fleet in fiscal Q1 2027.\n    Quote: And Oracle's GPU fleet ran at 97.9% utilization, meaning nearly every chip was busy serving customers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4331; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4571 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks), reporting Colette Kress's remarks; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:0c19765ee99d1f808e0834ec 2026-09-12] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA's compute was fully utilized across every cloud it serves.\n    Quote: She also said Nvidia's compute was running fully utilized across every cloud it serves.\n- e4572 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks), reporting Colette Kress's remarks; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:0c19765ee99d1f808e0834ec 2026-09-12] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress estimated revenue opportunity per gigawatt at about $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell, and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: A gigawatt of its older Hopper systems meant about $18 billion of revenue opportunity for Nvidia, chief financial officer Colette Kress said on the company's late-August earnings call. Blackwell runs about $25 billion, and the new Vera Rubin platform is about $40 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3375; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4574 [third_party_view; Daniel Sparks (fool.com); original source Daniel Sparks (fool.com) 2026-09-12; from gnews:0c19765ee99d1f808e0834ec 2026-09-12] Daniel Sparks judged Oracle's quarter to be strong customer-side evidence that NVIDIA is constrained by manufacturing capacity rather than demand.\n    Quote: Ultimately, though, I think it's the best customer-side evidence yet that what limits Nvidia is manufacturing capacity rather than appetite.\n- e4640 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Seaport Global ; from gnews:7708a5ba7485f4ee381ccc41 2026-09-14] Seaport Global argued that NVIDIA was sold out of capacity and might struggle to produce further upside surprises.\n    Quote: some, such as those from Seaport Global, have argued that as NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) is sold out of capacity, it might find it difficult to generate capacity for additional upside surprises.\n- e4725 [third_party_view; InsiderMonkey; original source InsiderMonkey 2026-09-15; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] InsiderMonkey reported that Anthropic had agreed to purchase billions of dollars of AI servers from AMD and that Broadcom was involved in efforts to develop custom AI chips for Anthropic.\n    Quote: More recently, Anthropic agreed to purchase billions of dollars of AI servers from AMD, while Broadcom has been involved in efforts to develop custom AI chips for Anthropic.\n- e4765 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA management said ACIE, about 45% of Data Center revenue, would drive most sequential Data Center revenue growth in fiscal Q3 2027, while hyperscaler demand could accelerate more in Q4 and FY2028.\n    Quote: ACIE already accounts for about 45% of Nvidia's Data Center revenue. Management expects ACIE to drive most of the sequential revenue growth for Data Center in the third quarter, while hyperscaler demand could pick up more strongly in the fourth quarter and in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4052; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4778 [fact; SCMP.com; original source Huawei Technologies ; from gnews:f19d2672d18ad2f26e5adeb3 2026-09-16] Huawei introduced near-packaged optics to its SuperPoD architecture for the first time at Huawei Connect 2026, changing the configuration of a competing AI-computing system.\n    Quote: At the Huawei Connect 2026 conference in Shanghai, the Shenzhen-based firm introduced near-packaged optics (NPOs) – a placement structure enabling higher speeds and lower energy use – to the company’s SuperPoD architecture for the first time.\n- e4779 [fact; SCMP.com; original source Huawei Technologies ; from gnews:f19d2672d18ad2f26e5adeb3 2026-09-16] Huawei unveiled its Atlas 960 SuperPoD computing cluster and an upgraded UnifiedBus interconnect at Huawei Connect 2026, introducing competing AI-computing products relevant to NVIDIA’s Data Center business.\n    Quote: Huawei Technologies on Thursday unveiled its latest Atlas 960 SuperPoD computing cluster and an upgraded version of its UnifiedBus interconnect technology, key components of the Chinese tech giant’s strategy to build advanced artificial intelligence systems despite US semiconductor-related restrictions.\n- e4783 [fact; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center segment revenue of $89.02 billion, up 117% year over year.\n    Quote: Data Center alone at $89.02 billion, up 117%\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3851; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4803 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:147e79a4a1f10279da449944 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 total revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, with about 92% from its Data Center business.\n    Quote: The quarter those numbers sit inside was itself enormous. Companywide revenue totaled $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, and about 92% of it came from the data center business.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4569; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4806 [fact; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:bc6926bf31d3a4d8006dce46 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n    Quote: NVIDIA's Data Center business remains the primary growth driver, generating $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 117% from a year ago and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4712; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4811 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Nokia ; from gnews:3c52e58b83c308775f689177 2026-09-16] Nokia said its AI-RAN platform is advancing from evaluation to live field trials at eight global operators; the report did not name the operators in this statement.\n    Quote: Nokia ( NYSE:NOK ) stock is rising in early Wednesday trading, up 6% to $10.39 after the company said its artificial intelligence-powered radio access network (AI-RAN) platform is moving from evaluation to live field trials at eight global operators.\n- e4814 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9a8cf969d7dedc50100397a 2026-09-16] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion in its latest quarter, about 92.5% of total sales.\n    Quote: Data-center revenue reached $89 billion , roughly 92.5% of Nvidia's latest quarterly sales,\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4740; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4816 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst), relaying The Information; original source Apple and NVIDIA ; from gnews:c25570f36f1535b739bebae6 2026-09-16] Apple has held talks with NVIDIA about using NVLink Fusion to connect chips in its planned AI server.\n    Quote: has held talks with Nvidia Corp. ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) about using its NVLink Fusion interconnect to link those chips\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4846; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4818 [fact; Zacks.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported FY2027 second-quarter revenue growth of 106% year over year and 18% sequentially; Data Center revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n    Quote: NVIDIA's second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, while Data Center revenue jumped 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3215, e4897; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4836 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:106a61a1ef10fb3ba6adb1cd 2026-09-16] Management expected Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of NVIDIA Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: management expects that platform to be