expects AI-related capital expenditures to reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030.\n    Quote: Nvidia expects to reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030.\n- e4849 [party_statement; benzinga.com; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:814422423846b7b421275ebe 2026-09-16] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the industry ultimately needs 50, 100 or even 1,000 neoclouds, compared with five hyperscalers.\n    Quote: Huang said Nvidia would be happy with five hyperscalers, but argued the industry ultimately needs 50, 100 or even 1,000 neoclouds.\n- e4850 [party_statement; benzinga.com; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:814422423846b7b421275ebe 2026-09-16] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA is helping regional cloud partners bring additional gigawatts of capacity online in Australia and Southeast Asia.\n    Quote: The strategy is already moving beyond the U.S. Huang pointed to activity in Australia and Southeast Asia, saying Nvidia is helping bring additional gigawatts of capacity online through regional cloud partners.\n- e4864 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Emerald AI, Google, and NVIDIA ; from gnews:4bf9544d82b0d4574011d0a2 2026-09-17] Emerald AI formed a coalition with Google and NVIDIA to use Emerald AI’s technology to secure grid capacity for more data centers.\n    Quote: Grid software unicorn Emerald AI has formed a coalition with Google and Nvidia that will use the startup’s technology to secure space on the grid for more data centers.\n- e4865 [third_party_view; TechCrunch; original source AI Energy Management Alliance (AEMA) ; from gnews:4bf9544d82b0d4574011d0a2 2026-09-17] The AI Energy Management Alliance said pausing or shifting data-center compute loads could allow an additional 100 GW of data centers to connect to the grid.\n    Quote: Pausing noncritical tasks and shifting some compute loads, AEMA says, could allow an additional 100 gigawatts of data centers to connect to the grid.\n- e4877 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source Huawei ; from gnews:509f491c104308dfdd480587 2026-09-17] Huawei plans to launch its Ascend 960DT in early 2027, about three quarters earlier than previously expected.\n    Quote: Huawei now plans to launch its Ascend 960DT in early 2027roughly three quarters earlier than previously expected\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4894, e5040; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4878 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source Huawei ; from gnews:509f491c104308dfdd480587 2026-09-17] Huawei plans to launch its Ascend 960PR in the third quarter of 2027, following the 960DT.\n    Quote: followed by the 960PR in the third quarter\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5041; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4879 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source Huawei ; from gnews:509f491c104308dfdd480587 2026-09-17] Huawei says its production remains insufficient to satisfy demand in China.\n    Quote: Huawei says production still cannot satisfy demand inside China\n- e4880 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:509f491c104308dfdd480587 2026-09-17] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion, about 92.5% of its latest-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion.\n    Quote: Data-center revenue reached $89 billion , accounting for roughly 92.5% of the company's latest $96.2 billion quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4352; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4882 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Rackspace Technology ; from gnews:83d2170d7a2b0ef2d1fb730a 2026-09-17] Rackspace entered NVIDIA's Cloud Partner Program to deliver and manage NVIDIA-powered AI computing for regulated and sovereign enterprises; no date or financial commitment was specified.\n    Quote: The company has entered Nvidia's Cloud Partner Program, aiming to strengthen its role in delivering and managing Nvidia-powered AI computing for regulated and sovereign enterprises.\n- e4883 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Rackspace Technology ; from gnews:83d2170d7a2b0ef2d1fb730a 2026-09-17] Rackspace introduced its Institutional Sovereign Pod, combining NVIDIA Blackwell technology with Palantir's Foundry and AIP platforms to support governed AI infrastructure; no date or scale was specified.\n    Quote: Alongside the partnership, Rackspace Technology also introduced its Institutional Sovereign Pod, an architecture that combines Nvidia's Blackwell technology with Palantir's Foundry and AIP platforms to support governed AI infrastructure.\n- e4894 [party_statement; Huawei spokesperson, quoted by TechCrunch; original source Huawei 2026-09-17; from gnews:d95188870b47d6bc96119e87 2026-09-17] Huawei said its Ascend 960DT AI chip is expected to be ready in Q1 2027, ahead of its previously planned Q3 2027 launch.\n    Quote: “The Ascend 960DT is expected to be ready in Q1 2027. Ascend 960 chips are launching ahead of schedule, doubling performance and advancing year by year,” the spokesperson said\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4877; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4895 [third_party_view; Rui Ma, quoted from her post on X by TechCrunch; original source Rui Ma 2026-09-17; from gnews:d95188870b47d6bc96119e87 2026-09-17] China tech analyst Rui Ma said Huawei’s newly announced Atlas 960 SuperPoD, described as using 4,096 Ascend 960 chips, is much smaller than the previously stated 15,488-chip configuration.\n    Quote: “The chip itself is coming WAY earlier, but the SuperPoD they announced is much smaller than what they originally laid out,” Ma wrote.\n- e4897 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:824eb11f9b37d2a3489d6e59 2026-09-18] Fool.com’s analyst reported that NVIDIA’s second-quarter revenue was $96 billion, up 106% year over year, and Data Center revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year; the quarter’s fiscal year is not specified.\n    Quote: The company recently reported terrific second-quarter results, with revenue up 106% year over year to $96 billion, and revenue from data centers up 117% to $89 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4818; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4915 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] NVIDIA's Data Center segment generated about $89 billion in revenue in the quarter ended July 26, 2026.\n    Quote: About $89 billion of that came from its data center segment.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3215; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4916 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), recounting Colette Kress's earnings-call remarks; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said hyperscale revenue was $49 billion, up 13% quarter over quarter, and ACIE revenue was $40 billion, up 25%, in the quarter ended July 26, 2026.\n    Quote: Hyperscale revenue was $49 billion, up 13% from the prior quarter, while the ACIE side grew even faster to $40 billion, up 25%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4051; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4927 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Nscale ; from gnews:f730ea7bda284baea7749a1c 2026-09-18] Nscale filed publicly for a New York IPO, saying it plans to acquire infrastructure to meet global AI-compute demand.\n    Quote: London-based AI data center company Nscale has filed publicly for an initial public offering (IPO) in New York to acquire the infrastructure needed to meet the global demand for artificial intelligence compute power.\n- e4928 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:f730ea7bda284baea7749a1c 2026-09-18] Bloomberg reported that Nscale is seeking to raise up to $3 billion in its IPO; Nscale did not disclose the offering's size in its filings.\n    Quote: The company did not disclose the size and scope of its listing in its filings. However, a Bloomberg report cited that the company is seeking to raise as much as $3 billion in the IPO.\n- e4929 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Nscale ; from gnews:f730ea7bda284baea7749a1c 2026-09-18] Nscale reported revenue of $140.6 million for the six months ended June 30, 2026, versus $10.4 million in the same period of 2025.\n    Quote: For the six-month period ending June 30, the company generated $140.6 million in revenue, up significantly from $10.4 million during the same period in the prior year.\n- e4930 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Nscale ; from gnews:f730ea7bda284baea7749a1c 2026-09-18] Nscale reported a net loss of $1.02 billion for the six months ended June 30, 2026, compared with $368.9 million in the same period of 2025.\n    Quote: However, net losses expanded to $1.02 billion for the period, compared to $368.9 million in the previous year.\n- e4931 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Nscale ; from gnews:f730ea7bda284baea7749a1c 2026-09-18] Nscale reported control of more than 10 gigawatts of power capacity across its AI hubs as of August 2026.\n    Quote: Nscale manages hyperscale AI hubs located in Norway, Portugal, Texas, and West Virginia, controlling over 10 gigawatts of power capacity as of August 2026.\n- e4932 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Nscale ; from gnews:f730ea7bda284baea7749a1c 2026-09-18] Nscale reported about $103 billion in total contract value.\n    Quote: The firm holds about $103 billion in total contract value.\n- e4933 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Nscale ; from gnews:f730ea7bda284baea7749a1c 2026-09-18] Nscale has a $45 billion agreement for Anthropic to use compute resources at its Monarch Compute Campus in West Virginia.\n    Quote: Major commitments include a $45-billion agreement with Anthropic to utilize compute resources at Nscale's flagship Monarch Compute Campus in West Virginia.\n- e4934 [third_party_view; Billy Duberstein; original source Billy Duberstein 2026-09-18; from gnews:4d4063796374226712c68282 2026-09-18] Motley Fool analyst Billy Duberstein judged that optical connectivity technology would outgrow Nvidia and others in the space over the next few years.\n    Quote: However, one particular connectivity technology is poised to outgrow everyone in the space over the next few years, including Nvidia.\n- e4935 [third_party_view; Kathy Ta, quoted by Billy Duberstein; original source Kathy Ta, Lumentum VP of Investor Relations ; from gnews:4d4063796374226712c68282 2026-09-18] Lumentum VP of Investor Relations Kathy Ta estimated that scale-across and scale-out optical markets were each about 10 times the data-center-interconnect market; initial scale-up CPO was expected from the second half of 2027 at 3–4 times the scale-out market, full optical scale-up at 10 times scale-out, and scale-in at another 10 times.\n    Quote: If we think of the Data Center Interconnect or the telecom market being sort of a 1x multiple, what we are seeing now for scale-across is probably 10x of what we saw for DCI. What we are seeing now for scale-out is probably 10x of what we are seeing for DCI. What we are seeing for scale-up, starting in the second half of 2027 for the first instance of CPO [co-packaged optics] going to scale-up, is\n- e4936 [third_party_view; Billy Duberstein; original source Billy Duberstein 2026-09-18; from gnews:4d4063796374226712c68282 2026-09-18] Billy Duberstein calculated that Lumentum VP Kathy Ta's bullish scenario of fully optical scale-up would imply 100-fold total addressable market growth, combining 10-fold scale-across and another 10-fold scale-up.\n    Quote: If all \"scale-up\" goes fully optical in the bullish scenario she envisioned, or another 10 times on top of the 10 times growth in scale-across, that's a total addressable market growth of 100 times.\n- e4968 [party_statement; Jim Cramer, quoted by Yahoo Finance from his CNBC remarks; original source Hock Tan, Broadcom CEO 2026-09-17; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan said demand for AI-development and inference compute infrastructure was extremely strong and durable; the item attributes the remark to his Mad Money appearance the previous night.\n    Quote: We see the demand for compute infrastructure for development of AI and inference as extremely strong and durable.\n- e4969 [third_party_view; Jim Cramer, quoted by Yahoo Finance from his CNBC remarks; original source Jim Cramer 2026-09-18; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Jim Cramer said Broadcom CEO Hock Tan had more orders than almost anybody other than NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang.\n    Quote: this man has more orders than almost anybody other than Jensen Huang.\n- e4970 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Broadcom 2026-09-02; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom reported that quarterly revenue grew 86% year over year and AI semiconductor revenue grew 221%; the item does not specify the quarter’s end date.\n    Quote: during the quarter, the firm's revenue grew by 86%, AI semiconductor revenue jumped by 221%\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4002; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4971 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Broadcom 2026-09-02; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom’s FY2026 guidance implied 186% annual growth in AI revenue.\n    Quote: fiscal year 2026 guidance implied 186% annual AI revenue growth.\n- e4972 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Hock Tan, Broadcom CEO ; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan reaffirmed that Broadcom could generate $115 billion in annual AI chip sales in 2027 and $230 billion in 2028.\n    Quote: CEO Tan reaffirmed that Broadcom Inc. (NASDAQ:AVGO) could pull in $115 billion in annual AI chip sales in 2027 and a whopping $230 billion in 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4532; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4973 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Broadcom 2026-09-02; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom guided to a 73% gross margin for fiscal Q4, five percentage points below a year earlier, citing a higher mix of XPU sales.\n    Quote: the firm guided Q4 gross margin at 73% which marked a five point annual drop due to a higher mix of XPU sales.\n- e4974 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance; original source Yahoo Finance 2026-09-18; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Estimates cited by Yahoo Finance suggested that OpenAI and Anthropic could account for 71% of Broadcom’s fiscal 2027 and 2028 XPU deployment.\n    Quote: estimates have suggested that 71% of Broadcom Inc. (NASDAQ:AVGO) 's fiscal 2027 and 2028 XPU deployment could rely on OpenAI and Anthropic\n- e4975 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Dario Amodei, Anthropic CEO ; from gnews:d7c05e7b0777edd1a79bc806 2026-09-18] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei said safety concerns necessitated a slowdown in AI development; the item gives no date or quantified extent.\n    Quote: His remarks followed Anthropic's Dario Amodei, who outlined that safety concerns necessitated a slowdown\n- e4976 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:d7c05e7b0777edd1a79bc806 2026-09-18] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said AI development did not have to slow down; the item gives no date or quantified extent.\n    Quote: Jensen Huang insisted that AI development did not have to slow down.\n- e4977 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:d7c05e7b0777edd1a79bc806 2026-09-18] Jensen Huang said AI labs with safety concerns should delay product launches until those 