ess.\n- e4983 [fact; Zacks Investment Ideas author; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:64b3da817a58392d7d1677e7 2026-09-18] Anthropic signed a $13.7 billion compute agreement earlier in September 2026.\n    Quote: Earlier this month, Anthropic inked a $13.7 billion compute agreement\n- e4984 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner estimated that Anthropic, OpenAI and xAI had combined annualized revenue of roughly $100 billion, based on figures circulating in July 2026.\n    Quote: Gerstner estimated that Anthropic, OpenAI and SpaceX (NASDAQ: SPCX ), which owns xAI, had a combined run rate of roughly $100 billion based on figures circulating in July.\n- e4985 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said Anthropic, OpenAI and xAI need combined annualized revenue of at least $180 billion by the end of 2026 to sustain the AI trade.\n    Quote: Gerstner said the companies need to reach at least $180 billion by year-end, up about 80% from his July estimate, \"just to keep the AI trade intact.\"\n- e4986 [rumour; benzinga.com; original source Unnamed sources cited in the reported figures ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Anthropic's reported annualized revenue run rate reached $65 billion in July 2026, up from $47 billion in May 2026.\n    Quote: Anthropic's revenue run rate reportedly reached $65 billion in July, up from $47 billion in May.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4987 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said Anthropic's reported $65 billion annualized revenue run rate fell short of investors' roughly $75 billion expectation.\n    Quote: He said stocks later consolidated after the $65 billion figure fell short of the roughly $75 billion investors had expected.\n- e4995 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA generated $164 billion in Data Center segment revenue in the first six months of FY2027.\n    Quote: The company has generated $164 billion in revenue from its data center segment in the first six months of fiscal 2027.\n- e4996 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-18; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] The author calculated an annualized Data Center revenue run rate of just under $330 billion from NVIDIA’s first-half FY2027 revenue.\n    Quote: That translates into an annual run rate of just under $330 billion for the full year.\n- e4999 [fact; TechRadar; original source Emerald AI, Google and NVIDIA ; from gnews:78a58240705bb94d9382a5dd 2026-09-18] Emerald AI, Google and NVIDIA formed the AI Energy Management Alliance to address data-center energy consumption.\n    Quote: Emerald AI, Google and Nvidia have come together to form the AI Energy Management Alliance (AEMA), and it could be exactly what we need to tackle rising energy consumption within the data center field.\n- e5000 [rumour; TechRadar, citing SiliconANGLE; original source SiliconANGLE ; from gnews:78a58240705bb94d9382a5dd 2026-09-18] Anthropic is reported to be planning to join the AI Energy Management Alliance as an additional partner.\n    Quote: SiliconANGLE reports that Anthropic will be one of them.\n- e5002 [fact; Zacks.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:57a69374b8fa083ce36ecfb1 2026-09-18] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion in FY2027 Q2, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n    Quote: The Data Center business remained the key growth driver, with revenues rising 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4712; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5035 [party_statement; NVIDIA Vice President Ian Buck, quoted in the article; original source Ian Buck ; from gnews:5a42294e229a65208f691101 2026-09-19] NVIDIA Vice President Ian Buck said AgentX benchmark computational demand rose 100-fold on purely numerical tasks; no period was specified.\n    Quote: In the AgentX benchmark, computational demand has increased by a factor of 100 on purely numerical tasks.\n- e5036 [party_statement; NVIDIA Vice President Ian Buck, quoted in the article; original source Ian Buck ; from gnews:5a42294e229a65208f691101 2026-09-19] Ian Buck said hourly rental rates for NVIDIA A100 instances have risen rather than declined over time, and described demand as remaining robust; no period or rate was specified.\n    Quote: In fact, if you track the hourly instance rental rates for the A100—released at the onset of the COVID‑19 pandemic—you'll find that they have not declined over time but have instead risen, with demand remaining robust.\n- e5040 [fact; korben.info; original source Huawei ; from gnews:4d60cc40cc0b61c1a9c9fb5d 2026-09-19] Huawei announced that its Ascend 960DT would launch as early as Q1 2027, nine months ahead of plan.\n    Quote: the Ascend 960DT, would launch as early as Q1 2027, nine months earlier than planned\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4877; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5041 [fact; korben.info; original source Huawei ; from gnews:4d60cc40cc0b61c1a9c9fb5d 2026-09-19] Huawei announced that the Ascend 960PR variant would follow in Q3 2027.\n    Quote: with the 960PR variant following in Q3.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4878; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5042 [party_statement; korben.info; original source Huawei ; from gnews:4d60cc40cc0b61c1a9c9fb5d 2026-09-19] Huawei said its planned Atlas 960 SuperPoD would link more than 4,000 accelerators via fiber optics to compensate for the Ascend chips’ performance gap with U.S. competitors.\n    Quote: its Atlas 960 SuperPoD, a cluster of more than 4,000 accelerators linked together via fiber optics, is meant to compensate through sheer numbers for what each chip concedes to the American company.\n- e5043 [party_statement; korben.info; original source Huawei ; from gnews:4d60cc40cc0b61c1a9c9fb5d 2026-09-19] Huawei said it was not producing enough Ascend chips to meet Chinese demand.\n    Quote: The group even admits it isn't producing enough to meet Chinese demand.\n- e5044 [party_statement; korben.info; original source Huawei ; from gnews:4d60cc40cc0b61c1a9c9fb5d 2026-09-19] Huawei outlined plans for Ascend 970 in 2028 and Ascend 980 in 2029, saying each generation would double performance.\n    Quote: Huawei is already promising what's next: an Ascend 970 in 2028, then a 980 in 2029, with a doubling of performance at each generation.\n- e5045 [fact; korben.info; original source Huawei ; from gnews:4d60cc40cc0b61c1a9c9fb5d 2026-09-19] Huawei is open-sourcing its CANN software, which the article says is already compatible with widely used tools such as PyTorch.\n    Quote: the company is also open-sourcing its in-house equivalent, CANN, already compatible with widely used tools like PyTorch.\n- e5058 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5feec912090c2af6467eed38 2026-09-19] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, including $89 billion of Data Center revenue.\n    Quote: Nvidia's revenue in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 (ended July 26) rose 106% year over year to $96.2 billion, with $89 billion of that coming from data centers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4569; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5072 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e5b9c11e2c8ef9754946a897 2026-09-20] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89.0 billion in Q2 FY2027, up 117% year over year.\n    Quote: Data center revenue climbed 117% to $89.0 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4590; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5097 [party_statement; moomoo.com, relaying Nscale’s prospectus; original source Nscale ; from gnews:a7ac49717831582bc5bb87db 2026-09-20] Nscale said that at the end of August 2026, its in-house data centers hosted 7 MW of active computing capacity and 48 MW operated in leased data-center space, under contracts totaling $2.6 billion.\n    Quote: Nscale stated that, as of the end of August this year, its in-house data centers hosted 7 megawatts of active computing capacity, while an additional 48 megawatts were operating in leased data center space, under contracts totaling $2.6 billion.\n- e5098 [party_statement; moomoo.com, relaying Nscale’s prospectus; original source Nscale ; from gnews:a7ac49717831582bc5bb87db 2026-09-20] Nscale said it was preparing 1.3 GW of data-center capacity; the item gives no completion date.\n    Quote: Furthermore, 1.3 gigawatts of capacity is currently being prepared.\n- e5099 [party_statement; moomoo.com, relaying Nscale’s estimate; original source Nscale ; from gnews:a7ac49717831582bc5bb87db 2026-09-20] Nscale estimated that its West Virginia data center, under construction, would begin phased commissioning by the end of 2027 and cost about $70 billion, including a 2 GW natural-gas-fired power plant.\n    Quote: Nscale estimates that this data center, currently under construction, is expected to begin phased commissioning by the end of next year, with a total cost of approximately US$70 billion, including a 2 GW natural-gas-fired power plant to support its operations.\n\n[Themes in force, 9]\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference sits at rung 2: NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance assumes no China Data Center compute revenue. That is a sales assumption, not a forecast that Chinese authorities will authorize no purchases. The evidence implies narrow, case-by-case authorization is more likely than no authorization by the\n- v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote.\n- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\n- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The status-quo reference is rung 2, and the evidence still makes no ban by the deadline more likely than a ban. Taiwan is considering a criminal ban, and the alleged export case—along with lawmakers’ and experts’ stated view that the law needs to change—adds pressure toward legislation. But the supp\n- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the investigation is formally underway and described as likely to remain an ongoing point of contention, with no Commerce deadline or consensus supplied. The access allegations and Taiwanese smuggling enforcement show the issue remains active, but they do not report a Commer\n- v41 (fed-policy-rate, due 2026-09-30) Will the Federal Reserve raise its policy rate at its September 2026 meeting?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is the fed funds futures market's 86% probability of an increase, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The later fact says the Federal Reserve raised interest rates on September 17, so the outcome at the meeting is Yes; this is not merely a forecast based on the reference.\n- v43 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-06-30) Will the required U.S. and EU regulatory reviews of NVIDIA's Hugging Face acquisition be cleared by 2027-06-30?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The stated completion plan falls within the deadline and therefore implies clearance by 2027-06-30. The mandatory U.S. filing and The Register's call for intervention add some risk, but neither supplies a regulator's objection, a delay, or a specific reason the reviews will remain uncleared by the d\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four companies, which is rung 4. A direct four-hyperscaler estimate of $760 billion and tracking near $745 billion support an implied outcome around $750–$760 billion. Newer estimates of nearly $800 billion refer to the top five, not these fo\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is approximately $1.08 trillion for calendar 2027, which is rung 3. The implied estimate remains about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate four-to-five share proxy (about $730 billion of $800 billion) to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five est\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The current evidence implies about $385–390 billion: the first two quarters produced about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and Rubin orders lift the Q4 estimate modestly, but the 2027–2028 deployment plans are not all FY2027 revenue, and supply availability limits the upside.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '/\"'^## The reading rule/,/''^'\"\\\\*\\\\*Reconciles\\\\*\\\\*/p' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/reading-rule.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '/\"'^## The reading rule/,/''^'\"\\\\*\\\\*Reconciles\\\\*\\\\*/p' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/reading-rule.md\"","aggregated_output":"## The reading rule (fixed before any answer)\nFor each answer, two readers (Claude first, then you blind to the arm under shuffled labels, given the answer, v14's 09-20 conclusion\nand the request's evidence) record:\n- **A.** Its FY2027 Data Center estimate and its Q3 and Q4 FY2027 Data Center estimates, as stated or as its own arithmetic gives them.\n- **B.** Whether its arithmetic adds up (its quarters sum to its year within $2B).\n- **C.** Whether each assumption is disclosed and grounded in its own request (a figure, guidance or growth rate that request\n  contains: the evidence listed, and v14's shown conclusion only in the changed arm); a step with no source in its request is\n  unsupported.\n- **D.** Room for the rest of NVIDIA: v14's figures minus the Data Center figures leave at least $25B for FY2027 and at least $6B\n  in each of Q3 and Q4. These floors are this test's assumptions, taken from the reported non-Data-Center revenue in the request\n  (Q1 $6.35B, Q2 $7.2B, first half $13.55B). Both arms are measured against v14's 09-20 figures ($419B FY, $113.5B Q3, $127B Q4),\n  whether or not the answer saw them.\n- **E.** (changed arm only, apart) whether v14's forecast bounds or changes its calculation (a v14 figure used in its arithmetic or\n  as a stated ceiling); a citation or a mention alone is recorded as \"noticed\", not \"used\".\n\n**Reconciles** = A gives all three figures, B, C and D all hold. A missing Q3 or Q4 estimate = \"not tested\", never a pass.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i '92[.]5|96[.]2|\\\\\"'$10[8-9]|110[.]16|414|411|'\"\\\\bQ[34]\\\\b|sequential|reacceler|second half|fiscal 2027|FY2027|\\\\\"'$12[057]|'\"\\\\\"'$130'\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/control-request-user.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i '92[.]5|96[.]2|\\\\\"'$10[8-9]|110[.]16|414|411|'\"\\\\bQ[34]\\\\b|sequential|reacceler|second half|fiscal 2027|FY2027|\\\\\"'$12[057]|'\"\\\\\"'$130'\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/control-request-user.txt\"","aggregated_output":"1:Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n2:Ladder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\n6:- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\n7:    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n19:- e976 [third_party_view; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n20:    Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\n21:- e2115 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar estimates NVIDIA Data Center revenue will exceed $300 billion in FY2027 and perhaps $500 billion in FY2028.\n22:    Quote: Nvidia should generate well over $300 billion of data center revenue in calendar 2026, which is effectively fiscal 2027, and perhaps over $500 billion in fiscal 2028.\n28:- e2517 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year.\n33:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e503 (2026-07-16); both are in force: Tom's Hardware reported that Rubin racks were expected to reach volume production in the second half of 2026; NVIDIA said the Noetra facility would support trillion-parameter model training as it expands, suggesting a phased ramp.\n37:- e2567 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA expected Vera Rubin to account for approximately 20% of Q3 FY2027 Data Center revenue.\n38:    Quote: We see Vera Rubin accounting for about 20% of data center revenue in Q3.\n43:- e2574 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000073:q2fy27cfocommentary.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported second-quarter FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, driven by the Blackwell Ultra ramp.\n44:    Quote: Data Center revenue for the second quarter was a record $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago and up 18% sequentially, driven by the ramp of our Blackwell Ultra infrastructure.\n49:- e2848 [party_statement; techcrunch.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided to third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, with some revenue expected from Rubin GPUs.\n50:    Quote: Nvidia said it expects revenue to reach $108 billion in the third quarter, some of which will come from its next-gen Rubin GPUs.\n51:- e3216 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source fool.com (Daniel Sparks) 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] NVIDIA Data Center revenue rose from 87.9% of total revenue in FY2026 Q2 to 92.5% in FY2027 Q2, increasing each quarter.\n52:    Quote: A year ago, in the second quarter of fiscal 2026, data center revenue was 87.9% of Nvidia's total. The share rose to 89.8% the next quarter, then 91.5%, then 92.2%, then 92.5% in the quarter just reported.\n53:- e3219 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source fool.com (Daniel Sparks) 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] Daniel Sparks calculated that FY2027 Q3 Data Center revenue would be $99.9 billion if its share of total revenue stayed at FY2027 Q2's 92.5%.\n54:    Quote: Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\n55:- e3222 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] NVIDIA said hyperscale-business growth should reaccelerate in FY2027 Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n56:    Quote: And the company said growth in its hyperscale business (sales to the giant cloud computing providers and the biggest consumer internet companies) should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n63:- e4052 [party_statement; calcalistech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] NVIDIA forecast most FY2027 Q3 data-center growth to come from ACIE, with hyperscale growth accelerating in Q4 and through FY2028 as Vera Rubin supply expands.\n75:- e4330 [party_statement; Benzinga, reporting Oracle CEO Clay Magouyrk's fiscal 2027 first-quarter earnings-call remarks; original source Clay Magouyrk, Oracle CEO ; from gnews:44f4bd6cc91ff33688f1e69f 2026-09-10] Oracle CEO Clay Magouyrk said Oracle had delivered 850 MW of AI capacity containing more than 300,000 GPUs since the end of the previous quarter.\n78:- e4331 [fact; Benzinga; original source Oracle ; from gnews:44f4bd6cc91ff33688f1e69f 2026-09-10] Oracle closed more than $30 billion in additional AI contracts during its fiscal 2027 first quarter without requiring additional capital; GPU utilization was 97.9%.\n110:- e4765 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA management said ACIE, about 45% of Data Center revenue, would drive most sequential Data Center revenue growth in fiscal Q3 2027, while hyperscaler demand could accelerate more in Q4 and FY2028.\n111:    Quote: ACIE already accounts for about 45% of Nvidia's Data Center revenue. Management expects ACIE to drive most of the sequential revenue growth for Data Center in the third quarter, while hyperscaler demand could pick up more strongly in the fourth quarter and in fiscal 2028.\n117:- e4783 [fact; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center segment revenue of $89.02 billion, up 117% year over year.\n120:- e4803 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:147e79a4a1f10279da449944 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 total revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, with about 92% from its Data Center business.\n121:    Quote: The quarter those numbers sit inside was itself enormous. Companywide revenue totaled $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, and about 92% of it came from the data center business.\n123:- e4806 [fact; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:bc6926bf31d3a4d8006dce46 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n124:    Quote: NVIDIA's Data Center business remains the primary growth driver, generating $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 117% from a year ago and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\n128:- e4814 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9a8cf969d7dedc50100397a 2026-09-16] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion in its latest quarter, about 92.5% of total sales.\n129:    Quote: Data-center revenue reached $89 billion , roughly 92.5% of Nvidia's latest quarterly sales,\n134:- e4818 [fact; Zacks.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported FY2027 second-quarter revenue growth of 106% year over year and 18% sequentially; Data Center revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n135:    Quote: NVIDIA's second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, while Data Center revenue jumped 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion.\n137:- e4836 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:106a61a1ef10fb3ba6adb1cd 2026-09-16] Management expected Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of NVIDIA Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n138:    Quote: management expects that platform to be about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n176:- e4880 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:509f491c104308dfdd480587 2026-09-17] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion, about 92.5% of its latest-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion.\n177:    Quote: Data-center revenue reached $89 billion , accounting for roughly 92.5% of the company's latest $96.2 billion quarter.\n183:- e4894 [party_statement; Huawei spokesperson, quoted by TechCrunch; original source Huawei 2026-09-17; from gnews:d95188870b47d6bc96119e87 2026-09-17] Huawei said its Ascend 960DT AI chip is expected to be ready in Q1 2027, ahead of its previously planned Q3 2027 launch.\n213:- e4935 [third_party_view; Kathy Ta, quoted by Billy Duberstein; original source Kathy Ta, Lumentum VP of Investor Relations ; from gnews:4d4063796374226712c68282 2026-09-18] Lumentum VP of Investor Relations Kathy Ta estimated that scale-across and scale-out optical markets were each about 10 times the data-center-interconnect market; initial scale-up CPO was expected from the second half of 2027 at 3–4 times the scale-out market, full optical scale-up at 10 times scale-out, and scale-in at another 10 times.\n214:    Quote: If we think of the Data Center Interconnect or the telecom market being sort of a 1x multiple, what we are seeing now for scale-across is probably 10x of what we saw for DCI. What we are seeing now for scale-out is probably 10x of what we are seeing for DCI. What we are seeing for scale-up, starting in the second half of 2027 for the first instance of CPO [co-packaged optics] going to scale-up, is\n229:- e4973 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Broadcom 2026-09-02; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom guided to a 73% gross margin for fiscal Q4, five percentage points below a year earlier, citing a higher mix of XPU sales.\n230:    Quote: the firm guided Q4 gross margin at 73% which marked a five point annual drop due to a higher mix of XPU sales.\n231:- e4974 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance; original source Yahoo Finance 2026-09-18; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Estimates cited by Yahoo Finance suggested that OpenAI and Anthropic could account for 71% of Broadcom’s fiscal 2027 and 2028 XPU deployment.\n232:    Quote: estimates have suggested that 71% of Broadcom Inc. (NASDAQ:AVGO) 's fiscal 2027 and 2028 XPU deployment could rely on OpenAI and Anthropic\n259:- e4995 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA generated $164 billion in Data Center segment revenue in the first six months of FY2027.\n260:    Quote: The company has generated $164 billion in revenue from its data center segment in the first six months of fiscal 2027.\n261:- e4996 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-18; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] The author calculated an annualized Data Center revenue run rate of just under $330 billion from NVIDIA’s first-half FY2027 revenue.\n267:- e5002 [fact; Zacks.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:57a69374b8fa083ce36ecfb1 2026-09-18] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion in FY2027 Q2, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n268:    Quote: The Data Center business remained the key growth driver, with revenues rising 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion.\n277:- e5041 [fact; korben.info; original source Huawei ; from gnews:4d60cc40cc0b61c1a9c9fb5d 2026-09-19] Huawei announced that the Ascend 960PR variant would follow in Q3 2027.\n278:    Quote: with the 960PR variant following in Q3.\n288:- e5058 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5feec912090c2af6467eed38 2026-09-19] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, including $89 billion of Data Center revenue.\n289:    Quote: Nvidia's revenue in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 (ended July 26) rose 106% year over year to $96.2 billion, with $89 billion of that coming from data centers.\n291:- e5072 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e5b9c11e2c8ef9754946a897 2026-09-20] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89.0 billion in Q2 FY2027, up 117% year over year.\n302:- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference sits at rung 2: NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance assumes no China Data Center compute revenue. That is a sales assumption, not a forecast that Chinese authorities will authorize no purchases. The evidence implies narrow, case-by-case authorization is more likely than no authorization by the\n312:[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The current evidence implies about $385–390 billion: the first two quarters produced about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and Rubin orders lift the Q4 estimate modestly, but the 2027–2028 deployment plans are not all FY2027 revenue, and supply availability limits the upside.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '205,314p' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/control-request-user.txt | awk '\"'!/''^    (Quote:|Opinion, not verified:)/'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
